of use cases is it good for?

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:24.500
BILLY JACOBSON: One of the big
uses is for a time series data,

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:25.470
like user analytics.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:29.850
So if you have your application
just tracking every interaction

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:31.590
people have on that,
thinking-- well,

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:33.090
Spotify is one of
the big customers,

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:36.750
so tracking every song that
gets listened to and storing

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:38.669
that in Bigtable,
all of those listens.

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:40.230
And then having
that data they can

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:41.940
run different kinds
of analytics on

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:44.130
and different kinds of
machine learning on.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.910
Once you have all
that time series data,

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:47.910
then you can do so many
different things with it

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:50.160
that you might not be able
to do with other databases.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.740
So running all kinds
of analytics on it,

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:54.698
doing different kinds
of personalization.

00:08:54.698 --> 00:08:56.490
And you can do that
all in real time, which

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:58.560
you can't necessarily
do with something

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.400
like BigQuery, where you can
take those queries and run them

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:03.120
ad hoc.

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.540
Bigtable let you set that up
to be used in an application,

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:09.612
and maybe create a dashboard
of all those analytics.

00:09:09.612 --> 00:09:11.320
Or when you're training
machine learning,

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:12.630
you can train
directly on the data.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:14.755
In Bigtable, you don't have
to move it to somewhere

00:09:14.755 --> 00:09:16.230
that can be accessed faster.

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:17.730
So all of those
different pieces are

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:19.410
why you might want
these Bigtable

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:21.055
in adjacent to
something like BigQuery.

00:09:21.055 --> 00:09:23.430
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So BigQuery
is more like the warehouse,

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:23.850
kind of?

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:25.320
You go and you
make these queries.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:28.000
They come back, you know,
usually pretty fast, seconds,

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.400
but that's still seconds.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.130
Whereas Bigtable, you're
placing it as a proper database,

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:34.340
and you can treat it like
one, and it works like one?

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:35.590
BILLY JACOBSON: Yeah, exactly.

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:38.130
So BigQuery is your
data warehouse.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:40.290
You might store the
data in both locations,

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:43.260
but you could give BigQuery
to your data analysts

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:47.082
and let them run queries
over the entire data set

00:09:47.082 --> 00:09:49.290
or even just play around
with different data sources.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.510
But really, if you're
doing a full table scan,

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:53.790
BigQuery would be
where you'd want to go.

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:57.330
Bigtable, you're going to want
to do different pieces of that.

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:59.860
So maybe get me all
the data for this user,

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:02.400
and then do training on it,
or display it on dashboard,

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:03.562
or something like that.

00:10:03.562 --> 00:10:05.520
MARK MANDEL: And what is
it about Bigtable that

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:08.370
makes that such a good fit?

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:11.220
What can it do that makes it
be able to do that so well?

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:12.875
BILLY JACOBSON:
So one of the ways

00:10:12.875 --> 00:10:14.250
Bigtable's able
to do all of this

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:16.420
is that it's a NoSQL database.

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.610
And because of that,
there's also no schema,

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:22.020
so-- well, there's one index,
which is just on the row key.

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.030
So you define your row key
based on a few properties.

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:29.010
So let's say you would set
maybe a timestamp, a user ID,

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:31.710
and some kind of other
categories in that row key.

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:34.980
Then you can only perform a
row get or a row range scan.

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:38.910
So you'd say, maybe, get me all
of the user data for this user

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:42.130
ID in this time range,
and maybe for this genre

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:43.320
or maybe for all genres.

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:45.030
And it would depend
on how you organize

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:46.680
those segments of that row key.

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:49.200
So it's basically just
a concatenated string

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:51.700
of a few of those
pieces of data.

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:55.300
So with BigQuery, you really
get that full SQL interface,

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:58.090
and it's really easy to
create queries, give it

00:10:58.090 --> 00:11:00.752
to some kind of
analyst, when Bigtable,

00:11:00.752 --> 00:11:02.460
you really have to
think about how you're

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:03.918
going to be using
it, setting it up

00:11:03.918 --> 00:11:06.090
for specific kinds of queries.

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:09.868
And once those are set up, those
are the only kinds of queries

00:11:09.868 --> 00:11:11.160
you're going to be able to run.

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:12.797
But they're going
to be super fast.

00:11:12.797 --> 00:11:14.880
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So by
putting a little bit more,

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:16.650
it sounds like thought,
into how you're

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:19.560
going to be arranging especially
the keys, but kind of how

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:22.170
the data is going to be put
into this database, that's

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:24.930
how you're able to keep
those really quick speeds up

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:26.190
and that huge scale.

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:27.065
BILLY JACOBSON: Yeah.

00:11:27.065 --> 00:11:29.620
With Bigtable you're getting
sub-10 millisecond latency.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:31.310
And Bigtable scales with nodes.

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:34.450
I said it's massively
scalable, so with each node

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:36.490
you get 10,000 QPS.

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:39.910
So you can just keep
adding more nodes.

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:44.860
So the typical minimum is three
nodes, which is 30,000 QPS,

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:47.920
but you can really scale
that up until there aren't

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:50.835
any more nodes left, like in all
of Google's cloud, which is--

00:11:50.835 --> 00:11:52.960
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Until
Google runs out of compute.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:53.250
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:53.350
BILLY JACOBSON: Yes.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:55.740
Until we run out of computers,
that's the end point.

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:56.020
MARK MIRCHANDANI: It's OK.

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:56.990
It's the cloud.

00:11:56.990 --> 00:11:57.500
MARK MANDEL: That's great.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:11:59.000
And just so I'm
100% clear as well,

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.500
I'm assuming you can do that
while things are live, as well?

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:03.820
I can scale that up and
down as my throughput

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:05.350
and my requirement change?

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:05.650
BILLY JACOBSON: Yeah.

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:07.160
So you can scale
that up and down.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:08.630
It's not going to
be instantaneous,

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:11.230
because some of the data
needs to rearrange itself

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:12.940
for you to see that benefit.

00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:15.980
I'm not totally sure how long
it takes, but it's not days.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:16.480
It's--

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:16.830
MARK MANDEL: Minutes?

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:18.610
BILLY JACOBSON:
Definitely minutes, yeah.

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:19.790
Definitely on that scale.

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:22.690
But back to those
row keys, if those

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:25.000
aren't distributed
evenly, then you

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:27.420
might run into some
performance issues.

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:29.230
But we've got a lot
of documentation

00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:32.020
and some code labs that
walk you through how

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:33.910
to think about those row keys.

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:35.590
And there are also
a bunch of talks

00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:37.990
that we've done at Next
showing sample row keys.

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:40.930
And we've got this great tool
called the Row Key Visualizer.

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:43.990
So you can maybe take
a subset of your data

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:45.640
and a subset of
your queries when

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:47.770
you're starting to plan
out what that schema might

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:51.240
look like and see, are
there any hotspots of data?

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:53.740
Are there any scans we're doing
a lot that don't look great?

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:57.460
We have a guide on what
query or what kind of imaging

00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:58.487
doesn't look great.

00:12:58.487 --> 00:12:59.320
So you can use that.

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:01.540
We've got that tool
to help you figure out

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:03.970
what schema, in quotes, is
going to be best for your data.

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:05.470
MARK MANDEL: And
so, also, like so we

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:07.600
were talking about it being
like a NoSQL store, as well.

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:09.020
So you've got your key, right?

00:13:09.020 --> 00:13:11.140
So that can be it basically
a concatenated string,

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:12.080
it sounds like?

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:12.340
BILLY JACOBSON: Yeah.

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:13.715
MARK MANDEL: And
then your value.

00:13:13.715 --> 00:13:16.720
Are we just dumping JSON
blobs in, or is there

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:17.680
a binary protocol, or--

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:18.490
BILLY JACOBSON: So we've got--

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:19.010
MARK MANDEL:
--whatever you like?

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:19.210
BILLY JACOBSON: Yeah.

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:21.770
So we've got column families
and column qualifiers.

00:13:21.770 --> 00:13:24.603
So the families do some
grouping on making it faster.

00:13:24.603 --> 00:13:26.770
If you're only querying
things from the same column,

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:28.380
families under the
column qualifiers

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:30.380
can help you map
to a specific cell.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:33.100
So you can just have a
bunch of cells with data.

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:36.118
And a cell is identified by
the row key, the column family,

00:13:36.118 --> 00:13:37.660
and the column
qualifiers-- just kind

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:40.480
of-- we just shortcut that
as the column, as well

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:41.410
as the timestamps.

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:44.177
You can multiple timestamps
within each cell.

00:13:44.177 --> 00:13:45.760
So that can make it
really good if you

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:49.000
want to use that for some part
of your time series management,

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.540
maybe saying, store an
hour of data in each cell,

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:54.933
or even just to have
just version tracking

00:13:54.933 --> 00:13:55.600
across the word.

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:58.090
But you don't want to use that
for your entire time series,

00:13:58.090 --> 00:13:59.757
because there's a
limit to how much data

00:13:59.757 --> 00:14:01.540
you can store per cell.

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:04.850
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So Bigtable
is a giant NoSQL database.

00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:07.390
Why would someone use
Bigtable over setting up

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:09.777
their own like Mongo
instance, for example?

00:14:09.777 --> 00:14:11.860
BILLY JACOBSON: Because
Bigtable is fully managed,

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:14.860
you're going to get the
security and the reliability

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:17.140
that Google Cloud is offering.

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:22.210
So we offer However many nines
of reliability for Bigtable.

00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:25.570
We have replication as a feature
so you can distribute your data

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:28.120
amongst the same
zone, or we also

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:29.950
have multiregion replication.

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:31.630
So you can really
distribute or data

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:34.710
and take advantage of
all the computing power

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:36.708
that Google Cloud has to offer.

00:14:36.708 --> 00:14:39.250
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Plus, as the
name implies, I hear it's big.

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:39.750
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:40.840
BILLY JACOBSON: Yeah.

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:41.340
And a table.

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:42.590
MARK MANDEL: And it's a table.

00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:44.860
[LAUGHS] You know, I think
you said something really

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:45.800
interesting there as well.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:47.758
You were talking about
it where not only can we

00:14:47.758 --> 00:14:49.660
do interzonal
communication, basically

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:51.220
like regional
failover, it sounds

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:52.750
like we can do global failover.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:53.470
Is that correct?

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:56.050
BILLY JACOBSON: Yeah, so you
can setup to have multiregion

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:56.840
replication.

00:14:56.840 --> 00:14:59.950
So if you're a global
business and want

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:02.930
to have your data stored
across all different regions

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:04.527
so it's easier
for you to access,

00:15:04.527 --> 00:15:05.860
you're going to get that faster.

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:07.240
You're going to
get lower latency

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:09.893
to people trying to access
data within those regions.

00:15:09.893 --> 00:15:11.560
MARK MANDEL: So it
sounds like your data

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:12.760
is essentially mirrored?

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:15.177
Or like you said, replicated,
I guess, around the globe.

00:15:15.177 --> 00:15:16.510
It's not just a failover system.

00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:18.678
You're actually making sure
you have multiple copies all

00:15:18.678 --> 00:15:18.870
over the world?

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:21.700
BILLY JACOBSON: Yeah, so you'll
be able to actually set up

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:24.610
different application
profiles in terms of where

00:15:24.610 --> 00:15:25.930
that data might get accessed.

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:29.440
You can specify that if your,
maybe, customers are in the US,

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:32.260
you could have them go towards
the US replication, access

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:32.980
data from that.

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:35.030
But you can set it up
in a few different ways.

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:37.360
If you want to be using the
replications as a backup,

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:40.600
then you can have those not
get accessed at all for any

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:43.060
reads or writes, and
only use that to store

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:44.440
the data as a backup.

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:46.570
Or you can have it where
you're continuously

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:49.750
interacting with both
instances of the replication.

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:52.340
It's going to be an
eventual consistent system

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:54.427
so you might have some
stale data that comes back.

00:15:54.427 --> 00:15:56.260
But it really just
depends on your use case.

00:15:56.260 --> 00:15:59.500
It's a very flexible
product, so really, you

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:02.350
need to figure out what parts
of it you're going to use

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:04.450
and how they apply
to your use case

00:16:04.450 --> 00:16:08.290
and turn all the knobs to
specifically for that use case.

00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:09.790
MARK MANDEL: Now
I'm wondering here,

00:16:09.790 --> 00:16:11.590
it sounds like I could
probably do something like,

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:13.840
maybe these kind of rows
that have this kind of data,

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:16.720
maybe replicate that in one way,
but these kinds of rows maybe

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:17.388
don't.

00:16:17.388 --> 00:16:18.430
Would that be right, or--

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:20.380
BILLY JACOBSON: Yes, I think
it's more of-- at least

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:21.302
at the table level.

00:16:21.302 --> 00:16:22.510
It might even be the entire--

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:23.170
MARK MANDEL: Oh,
that makes sense.

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:25.040
BILLY JACOBSON: I think it's of
the entire instance, actually.

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:25.860
MARK MANDEL: Yeah

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:27.690
BILLY JACOBSON: But we can
include the replication docs

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:28.660
in the show notes.

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:29.310
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:31.685
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So if you
were going to design a system

00:16:31.685 --> 00:16:33.420
and had your major
use base, let's just

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:34.920
say, being in the
US, but you wanted

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:38.080
to still have lower read latency
for people across the globe.

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:40.200
If you were then to
spin up a major writing

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:42.810
center in, let's
say, the APAC area,

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:45.030
could there be a
multimaster scenario here,

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:46.710
or would everyone
just be writing to one

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:48.780
and then replicating to
all these read replicas?

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:49.380
BILLY JACOBSON:
Yeah, I think there

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:50.730
could be multiple masters.

00:16:50.730 --> 00:16:52.900
From what I know, I
think that sounds OK.

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:55.080
I'd have to check in the
docs just to confirm.

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:56.640
MARK MANDEL: So I kind of want
to head over to the open source

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:58.410
side, too, which is
kind of interesting.

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:00.420
I know we support
like an HBase client.

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:03.390
Can you tell us about HBases as
well, and just how that works?

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:04.265
BILLY JACOBSON: Yeah.

00:17:04.265 --> 00:17:07.349
So this in the history
of how Bigtable formed.

00:17:07.349 --> 00:17:10.217
Google's been using
Bigtable internally for--

00:17:10.217 --> 00:17:12.050
basically since the
start of Google, almost.

00:17:12.050 --> 00:17:14.930
It's helped our search,
helped our Gmail,

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:17.460
and these core business
products that we have.

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:19.369
And I don't remember
which year, exactly,

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:21.780
but we published a white
paper that explained,

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:23.400
what is Bigtable?

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:25.030
Basically, how could
someone create it?

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:26.130
How we're using it?

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:28.770
And the open source
community took that paper

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:31.890
and created HBase, which
is the open source database

00:17:31.890 --> 00:17:33.180
version of this, basically.

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:34.920
And over time, some
of these features

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:38.170
have diverged from what
internal Bigtable has to offer.

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:40.530
And then a few years
after we created

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:43.710
this white paper and HBase came
out, we created Cloud Bigtable.

00:17:43.710 --> 00:17:45.810
So when we started
with Cloud Bigtable,

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:47.640
we wanted to make
sure that people

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:49.260
who were using
HBase and used to it

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:52.170
had an ability to just take
whatever they were using

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:56.490
and just swap it directly
out from HBase to Bigtable.

00:17:56.490 --> 00:17:59.040
So we support the HBase API.

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:01.080
So anyone who's familiar
with using HBase

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:04.200
should be able to just transfer
their stuff over pretty easily

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:06.750
and use all of the API
commands that they're used to

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:08.585
and not have to really
change their code.

00:18:08.585 --> 00:18:10.710
And because of the open
source community with HBase

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:12.530
and because we have
this HBase client,

00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:14.238
there are all these
open source libraries

00:18:14.238 --> 00:18:17.100
that we get to use for free
with Bigtable, just because

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:18.160
of that compatibility.

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:22.080
So one of them is JanusGraph,
which does graph databases.

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:24.990
There's OpenTSDB for
time series and GeoMesa

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:27.150
for geospatial data.

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:30.000
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So it kind of
fits pretty well into the cloud

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.730
modularity model, where you
can take these different pieces

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:34.020
and swap them out.

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:36.090
And this one happens
to be a great solution

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:38.702
for a fully-managed
database in the backend,

00:18:38.702 --> 00:18:40.410
but then your application,
wherever it is

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:42.090
or whatever it is,
could theoretically

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:44.400
swap this out and get all
those benefits without having

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:45.250
to rewrite anything?

00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:46.680
BILLY JACOBSON: Yeah, yeah.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:47.940
So it's really nice for that.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:49.813
But now, many years later--

00:18:49.813 --> 00:18:51.480
I mean, I know the
last time you all had

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:52.640
some talk about Bigtable was--

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:53.190
MARK MANDEL: [LAUGHS]
It's a while ago.

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:54.300
BILLY JACOBSON: --in 2016.

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:54.600
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:56.767
BILLY JACOBSON: So now we
actually have nine clients

00:18:56.767 --> 00:18:57.690
to use with Bigtable.

00:18:57.690 --> 00:18:59.950
We just came out
with a Java client.

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:02.580
So for people who want to
use a more idiomatic Java

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:04.497
client, as with the
other products we have,

00:19:04.497 --> 00:19:06.205
because they don't
have their own-- like,

00:19:06.205 --> 00:19:09.630
Spanner doesn't have an HBase or
open source equivalent client.

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:11.830
So now if you're using
other products that we have,

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:14.790
you'll be able to use a very
easy-to-use Java client.

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:17.280
And we have Python, Go.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:19.530
It's all the assorted languages
that we have to offer.

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:21.738
MARK MANDEL: So here's an
interesting question, then.

00:19:21.738 --> 00:19:24.780
Where do you think people should
choose, using either the GCP

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:27.250
native clients, or should
they use the HBase ones?

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:29.210
Or where does that
pro and con come from?

00:19:29.210 --> 00:19:31.710
BILLY JACOBSON: I think it's
just if you are using the HBase

00:19:31.710 --> 00:19:34.240
clients already, probably
stick with HBase clients.

00:19:34.240 --> 00:19:37.230
I know a lot of people,
even before we came out

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:40.823
with this Java client, had
created their own Java clients

00:19:40.823 --> 00:19:42.490
and just found that
easier to work with.

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:44.783
So we were like, oh,
let's do that, too.

00:19:44.783 --> 00:19:46.950
MARK MANDEL: So like
greenfield, to use one of ours,

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:48.910
is probably a bit of
a nicer experience.

00:19:48.910 --> 00:19:50.250
But if you already
have HBase, then maybe

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:50.940
go with that instead?

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:52.140
BILLY JACOBSON:
Yeah, definitely.

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:53.200
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And you
also mentioned-- you know,

00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:56.580
earlier we were talking about
Bigtable versus BigQuery.

00:19:56.580 --> 00:19:58.590
And so they're very
different solutions,

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:00.247
despite sounding
somewhat similar.

00:20:00.247 --> 00:20:01.080
BILLY JACOBSON: Yes.

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:03.330
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And then
you just mentioned Spanner.

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:05.910
So if someone's coming to
this from a fresh perspective,

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:09.030
when is a use case that you
might use Bigtable or Spanner,

00:20:09.030 --> 00:20:10.060
or vise versa?

00:20:10.060 --> 00:20:10.935
BILLY JACOBSON: Yeah.

00:20:10.935 --> 00:20:13.980
I think this is a very
common point of confusion,

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:16.470
because it really does
depend on your use case.

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:18.630
And Daniel Bergqvist
and I actually

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:20.940
gave a talk on this about
specifically internet

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:23.080
of things, at Google
I/O. So we should

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:24.330
link that in the description--

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:24.450
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:26.200
BILLY JACOBSON: --for
more of the details.

00:20:26.200 --> 00:20:28.830
But for Internet of Things, we
realized that a very common use

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:31.590
case for all three of these
types of databases or data

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:32.460
warehouses.

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:34.020
And I guess what
we were saying is,

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:36.660
if you have this
time series data

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:39.600
and you're looking at
these three solutions, what

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:42.930
might be helpful to consider
is typically, if you're just

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:45.150
trying to do something with
a time series database,

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:46.740
go with Bigtable.

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:50.320
That has all the basics you
need for a time series database.

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:54.030
It's really fast, can
scale, and do all of that.

00:20:54.030 --> 00:20:56.250
We also looked a little bit
Datastore and Firestore,

00:20:56.250 --> 00:20:59.670
and that's good at a smaller
scale with lower QPS.

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:02.582
And when you get into the
scale of it, with the Bigtable

00:21:02.582 --> 00:21:04.290
or with Spanner, you
have to play with it

00:21:04.290 --> 00:21:05.580
to figure that out.

00:21:05.580 --> 00:21:08.370
And then for Spanner, if
you have transactions.

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:12.030
That's really where if you're
using a time series database,

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:13.530
transactions are
really where you're

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:15.780
going to get the power of
using Spanner over something

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:16.710
like Bigtable.

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:19.570
And you also get the SQL
interface, which is nice,

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:21.390
so that might be
part of the reason.

00:21:21.390 --> 00:21:24.240
But typically it's because
of those transactions.

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:25.917
And with Spanner,
you're going to get--

00:21:25.917 --> 00:21:28.500
I believe the writes are going
to be slower with Spanner, just

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:30.630
because of the way the
data is replicated.

00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:34.920
So they have to write the
data to all the locations

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:37.650
that the data is replicated
before you can read from it,

00:21:37.650 --> 00:21:40.140
whereas Bigtable, because
it's eventually consistent,

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:42.340
you'll write it and then
you can read it right away.

00:21:42.340 --> 00:21:44.730
It just may not be there,
especially if it's replicated.

00:21:44.730 --> 00:21:45.330
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:47.038
BILLY JACOBSON: And
then for BigQuery, we

00:21:47.038 --> 00:21:49.830
kind of said, think about those
databases totally separately.

00:21:49.830 --> 00:21:52.980
So if you're going to choose
Datastore or Firestore,

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:55.710
Bigtable or Spanner, that's
one part of your solution.

00:21:55.710 --> 00:21:58.770
And then to use it, a
data warehouse, that's

00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:00.630
kind of like a
totally separate part.

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:02.880
So for that, you'd use BigQuery.

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:05.280
And like I said, or you
do those ad hoc queries,

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:07.130
you give that to a
data analyst, you

00:22:07.130 --> 00:22:09.960
can do full table scans, which
aren't really great for Spanner

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:10.980
or Bigtable.

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:12.610
That's why you
would use BigQuery.

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:13.410
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And
like you mentioned,

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:15.077
I mean, you might
have a system in place

00:22:15.077 --> 00:22:18.035
where you use both Bigtable
and BigQuery at the same time

00:22:18.035 --> 00:22:18.310
for different purposes.

00:22:18.310 --> 00:22:19.380
BILLY JACOBSON:
Yeah, it's a very--

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:19.880
yeah.

00:22:19.880 --> 00:22:20.830
So that's very common.

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:23.010
We typically see people
have a Dataflow job that

00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:26.130
writes to both at the
same time, or even takes

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:28.320
their data from BigQuery
and writes it over

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:30.773
to Bigtable, or vise versa.

00:22:30.773 --> 00:22:31.440
MARK MANDEL: Oh.

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:32.815
I know what I want
to talk about.

00:22:32.815 --> 00:22:34.000
[CHUCKLING]

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:37.140
So what's the local developer
experience like with Bigtable?

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:39.420
Do I have any emulation
tools or anything like that

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:40.865
that I can run
locally, so that I

00:22:40.865 --> 00:22:42.240
can play with this
without having

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:44.320
to spin up a bunch of
clusters in the cloud?

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:47.790
BILLY JACOBSON: So we
have Bigtable emulator.

00:22:47.790 --> 00:22:49.705
I haven't had a chance
to play too much, so--

00:22:49.705 --> 00:22:50.580
MARK MANDEL: [LAUGHS]

00:22:50.580 --> 00:22:51.390
BILLY JACOBSON:
--I can't answer--

00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:52.440
MARK MANDEL: It
is there, though.

00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:53.170
BILLY JACOBSON: So we have that.

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:53.520
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:54.937
BILLY JACOBSON:
And we also have--

00:22:54.937 --> 00:22:56.850
so Bigtable nodes
are fairly expensive.

00:22:56.850 --> 00:22:59.118
I think they're around 600, 650.

00:22:59.118 --> 00:23:00.660
I don't want to say
the exact number.

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:01.290
MARK MANDEL: We
can look into it.

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:03.240
BILLY JACOBSON: Per
node, so typically you

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:05.590
do with three nodes for
a production environment,

00:23:05.590 --> 00:23:07.290
so that can get pretty
costly per month.

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:09.510
And I think-- although,
when I was listening back

00:23:09.510 --> 00:23:13.510
to Ian's podcast, I think it was
triple that rate for one node.

00:23:13.510 --> 00:23:15.777
So that's at least great
we've come down on the price.

00:23:15.777 --> 00:23:16.652
MARK MANDEL: [LAUGHS]

00:23:16.652 --> 00:23:18.900
BILLY JACOBSON: But we do have
a development instance you can

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:20.240
use, so that's just one node.

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:20.430
MARK MANDEL: Oh, cool.

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:21.120
BILLY JACOBSON: So
that's a good way

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:22.870
to actually just play
with it in the cloud

00:23:22.870 --> 00:23:24.120
and actually get to use--

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:26.138
you're going to lose
out on some of the power

00:23:26.138 --> 00:23:27.930
by only having one
node, but you can really

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:29.760
start interacting
with it and get

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:31.105
a feel for the API and stuff.

00:23:31.105 --> 00:23:31.980
MARK MANDEL: So yeah.

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:34.800
I mean, as you said, it's been a
while since we talked about it.

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:37.110
What has changed in the last
two years with Bigtable?

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:39.570
We've got like new, cool
stuff, just more columns

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:40.300
and more keys?

00:23:40.300 --> 00:23:40.800
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:42.550
BILLY JACOBSON: Well,
I mean, at the base,

00:23:42.550 --> 00:23:44.880
Bigtable has stayed
the same, because I

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:47.460
think it's such a foundational
product it can't really

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:48.190
change too much.

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:49.630
But we've added the replication.

00:23:49.630 --> 00:23:51.030
We've added these new clients.

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:53.040
And we also have
this Key Visualizer.

00:23:53.040 --> 00:23:56.880
I think what's changed is the
environment around Bigtable.

00:23:56.880 --> 00:23:59.590
I mean, GCP has grown so
much, so there's more--

00:23:59.590 --> 00:24:01.680
if you want to store
stuff in Bigtable,

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:04.305
the rest of your ecosystem
can comfortably be in GCP,

00:24:04.305 --> 00:24:06.180
and you're going to find
all those ways you'd

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:08.340
want to interact
with Bigtable there.

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:12.230
So Dataflow jobs, Dataproc,
even, some of the ML

00:24:12.230 --> 00:24:14.790
features that we have in GCP.

00:24:14.790 --> 00:24:16.830
So really, it's all
about this environment

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:17.880
that's around Bigtable.

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:20.220
You want-- Bigtable, you're
getting that low latency,

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:22.220
so you don't want to have
your stuff in Bigtable

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:23.610
and then be doing
analytics on it

00:24:23.610 --> 00:24:24.990
somewhere else,
because then you're

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:26.615
going to lose some
of that low latency.

00:24:26.615 --> 00:24:28.860
So getting to have
an ecosystem that

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:31.740
supports Bigtable and
supports everything around it,

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:34.770
I think that's where GCP has
grown over the past few years.

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:36.520
MARK MANDEL: One of
my favorite questions.

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:38.760
What has been the most
interesting thing you've

00:24:38.760 --> 00:24:40.230
seen someone do with Bigtable?

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:41.650
Possibly weird or wacky.

00:24:41.650 --> 00:24:43.608
BILLY JACOBSON: One of
the things I really like

00:24:43.608 --> 00:24:45.420
is Spotify's Discover Weekly.

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:47.400
So that happens on Bigtable.

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:49.410
We've given a few talks
about how it works,

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:50.620
about their ecosystem.

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:53.460
And I think they were saying
before they had Bigtable,

00:24:53.460 --> 00:24:55.930
they'd have some
engineers up all night

00:24:55.930 --> 00:24:58.530
trying to make sure that their
recommendation algorithm wasn't

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:01.320
going to crash or was
going to be available

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:04.260
that Monday morning for those
Discover Weekly playlists.

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:06.390
And for them to
get on to Bigtable

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:09.180
and start using the
machine learning on that

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:11.340
and to have it to just be
not something they even

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:14.010
have to worry about anymore
and could bring more

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:16.630
recommendations and
different ways with that,

00:25:16.630 --> 00:25:20.260
I think that's just such a cool
thing that's impacted my life.

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:22.620
One of the cool ways I've
got to play with Bigtable

00:25:22.620 --> 00:25:26.280
was this past GCP
Next, or Next 2019.

00:25:26.280 --> 00:25:29.820
We were figuring out what
can we do to really try out

00:25:29.820 --> 00:25:32.850
a real Bigtable use case,
play with it in a way

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:34.800
that developers might
be playing with it?

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:36.210
So we were like,
where do we find

00:25:36.210 --> 00:25:39.450
like a cool Internet of Things
or time series database?

00:25:39.450 --> 00:25:41.580
And we were poking
around, and we

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:44.070
found this team called
Air View, and they've

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:46.870
put air quality sensors in the
back of Google Street View cars

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:47.370
and--

00:25:47.370 --> 00:25:47.490
MARK MANDEL: Oh, wow.

00:25:47.490 --> 00:25:49.050
BILLY JACOBSON: --have been
driving them around the Bay

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:50.830
Area and a few other cities.

00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:53.820
So we contacted them and started
streaming some of that data

00:25:53.820 --> 00:25:56.610
into Bigtable through
a Dataflow pipeline.

00:25:56.610 --> 00:26:00.070
And then we're able to create
a real visualization with it.

00:26:00.070 --> 00:26:02.170
So that was cool
just to see like, oh,

00:26:02.170 --> 00:26:04.040
if you've got like
an Internet of Things

00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:06.090
and a fleet of
Internet of Things,

00:26:06.090 --> 00:26:08.417
how that process
can be done and how

00:26:08.417 --> 00:26:10.500
you could use that to
create a real-time dashboard

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:12.370
and do real analytics on it.

00:26:12.370 --> 00:26:13.890
And while we only
had a few cars,

00:26:13.890 --> 00:26:15.570
it's cool to know
we could easily

00:26:15.570 --> 00:26:20.140
is to scale it up to a
thousand cars or 10,000 even

00:26:20.140 --> 00:26:21.930
and-- just by adding more nodes.

00:26:21.930 --> 00:26:23.370
MARK MANDEL: Is there anywhere
people should specifically

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:25.230
go if they want to learn
more about Bigtable?

00:26:25.230 --> 00:26:27.772
BILLY JACOBSON: I would say go
to the Bigtable documentation.

00:26:27.772 --> 00:26:29.360
We've got links to
a lot of the talks

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:31.700
we did at Next, which show off
some of these really cool use

00:26:31.700 --> 00:26:32.200
cases.

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:34.880
Some of the personalization,
some anti-fraud,

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:37.090
the time series
analysis, that talk

00:26:37.090 --> 00:26:39.770
at I/O about how to
choose your database.

00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:41.390
I think with
Bigtable, you really

00:26:41.390 --> 00:26:43.980
want to know how other
people are using it

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:46.640
so you can learn about
each knob and how to turn

00:26:46.640 --> 00:26:48.915
them to best benefit you.

