00:27:55.880 --> 00:27:57.570
So if I have an
organization, I've

00:27:57.570 --> 00:28:00.520
set up an organization for
my variety of GCP projects,

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:02.765
and my projects are
separated out by folder

00:28:02.765 --> 00:28:04.390
so I can manage them
in a coherent way,

00:28:04.390 --> 00:28:05.930
it's easy to see
what's going on,

00:28:05.930 --> 00:28:09.110
how do I break down my
billing data by folder

00:28:09.110 --> 00:28:11.530
so I can see what's going
on at that folder level?

00:28:11.530 --> 00:28:12.160
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right, yeah.

00:28:12.160 --> 00:28:13.952
I mean, this is a really
important question

00:28:13.952 --> 00:28:17.080
for people who are running an
organization inside of GCP.

00:28:17.080 --> 00:28:20.380
So a lot of people who are
just either individual users

00:28:20.380 --> 00:28:23.560
or hobbyists or something like
that can spin up Google Cloud

00:28:23.560 --> 00:28:25.180
Platform with their
personal accounts,

00:28:25.180 --> 00:28:27.790
and they can still set
up a billing account,

00:28:27.790 --> 00:28:29.980
export that billing
account to BigQuery,

00:28:29.980 --> 00:28:33.550
and then you have a
actual line-by-line more

00:28:33.550 --> 00:28:35.840
detailed version of
your billing data set.

00:28:35.840 --> 00:28:37.255
So you can write any analytics--

00:28:37.255 --> 00:28:37.510
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:38.300
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
--based on that,

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:39.842
or you can do any
work based on that.

00:28:39.842 --> 00:28:42.723
But when you're an organization,
maybe even like a Macy's,

00:28:42.723 --> 00:28:44.890
and your organization
structure is a little bit more

00:28:44.890 --> 00:28:48.350
complicated, you can create
folders to group your projects.

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:50.920
But when you export
that to BigQuery,

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:54.123
you only have the IDs of the
folders, not the actual names.

00:28:54.123 --> 00:28:54.790
MARK MANDEL: Oh.

00:28:54.790 --> 00:28:56.560
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And that can
be a big challenge, because,

00:28:56.560 --> 00:28:56.870
you know--

00:28:56.870 --> 00:28:57.703
MARK MANDEL: Got it.

00:28:57.703 --> 00:29:00.580
MARK MIRCHANDANI: --I may not
be looking for folder 1256734,

00:29:00.580 --> 00:29:03.130
I'm looking for folder
Production and Development.

00:29:03.130 --> 00:29:06.100
So Nick has written this cool
little article and a very, very

00:29:06.100 --> 00:29:10.000
quick tutorial to show you how
to use the open source GitHub

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:12.850
project GCP Folder Look
Up, which will basically

00:29:12.850 --> 00:29:14.680
take your organization,
take your folders,

00:29:14.680 --> 00:29:16.493
and put that out
to BigQuery table,

00:29:16.493 --> 00:29:18.160
and then you join
that with your billing

00:29:18.160 --> 00:29:21.550
data set to actually get
a more detailed view.

00:29:21.550 --> 00:29:23.380
Now all of your line
items will actually

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:26.180
have the organization
structure in them as well.

00:29:26.180 --> 00:29:28.360
So any analysis you're
doing with BigQuery

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:30.820
is a lot easier because you
can say, OK, well, give me

00:29:30.820 --> 00:29:33.252
all the costs and show
me to me by this folder,

00:29:33.252 --> 00:29:34.960
or show it to me by
this folder, but only

00:29:34.960 --> 00:29:36.503
for this specific GCP project.

00:29:36.503 --> 00:29:37.420
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:40.390
So this creates another
table inside your query data

00:29:40.390 --> 00:29:42.550
structures so that you
can then join that up

00:29:42.550 --> 00:29:44.120
based on the ID of that table?

00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:44.590
Would that-- is right?

00:29:44.590 --> 00:29:46.420
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Exactly, yeah,
with the name of the folder

00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:48.878
and then with the line items
that come through the BigQuery

00:29:48.878 --> 00:29:49.390
export.

00:29:49.390 --> 00:29:51.580
It's way, way more
powerful, and you

00:29:51.580 --> 00:29:54.760
can see every single individual
cost over any time span

00:29:54.760 --> 00:29:57.430
as soon as you start enabling
the BigQuery export, of course.

00:29:57.430 --> 00:30:01.390
So it's a great way to either do
data analytics on your billing

00:30:01.390 --> 00:30:04.130
data or even just to
look up information.

00:30:04.130 --> 00:30:05.950
You can also plug
it into Data Studio

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:07.840
and you get a nice,
cool visualization layer

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:08.930
on top of that.

00:30:08.930 --> 00:30:09.380
MARK MANDEL: Oh, that's nice.

00:30:09.380 --> 00:30:10.120
